# Gong API credentials
GONG_ACCESS_KEY=your_access_key_here
GONG_ACCESS_SECRET=your_access_secret_here

# Master secret for the HTTP endpoint. Required — with this unset the deployed
# endpoint refuses every request and the OAuth endpoints cannot sign anything.
# It is accepted directly as a bearer token, and it is the key the OAuth
# artifacts are signed with, so rotating it invalidates every issued token.
# The stdio entrypoint ignores it, being a local subprocess already.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Optional: the password typed on the OAuth consent screen. Defaults to
# MCP_AUTH_TOKEN, which works but means pasting 64 hex characters — including on
# a phone. Set something memorable here instead. It grants full read access to
# your Gong data, so treat it as a password, not a PIN.
# MCP_LOGIN_PASSWORD=

# ACCESS CONTROL ------------------------------------------------------------
# Gong's API key sees EVERY call in the workspace — private and internal ones
# included — and cannot be scoped per user. So the tools gate access here. A
# call is exposed only when it is a customer-facing sales/CS call. Two signals
# can define that; use either or both. With none set the gate is OFF and every
# call the key can see is exposed — set something before serving a team.
#
# (1) RECOMMENDED — require a known CRM account. A call is in scope only when a
# customer/prospect actually SPOKE on it AND Gong has linked it to a CRM
# account. This defines "customer" straight from your CRM (via Gong's CRM
# integration) and stays current automatically — no email list to maintain and
# no managers to exclude: internal/leadership calls aren't tied to a customer
# account, and a board member / investor / advisor / vendor isn't one either,
# so those all stay hidden. VERIFY Gong returns CRM context first — run a
# search and confirm matches carry an "accounts" field; if empty this hides
# everything. Accepts true/1/yes.
# GONG_REQUIRE_CRM_ACCOUNT=true
#
# (2) OPTIONAL — an explicit roster of your sales / CS / SDR / support reps.
# When set, a call must also include a roster member. Useful if you want to
# scope to GTM-team calls specifically, or as a fallback when CRM context is
# not available. Requiring a customer who actually SPOKE (not just a silent
# invitee/note-taker) is what keeps internal meetings out. User IDs are the
# stable match; emails are easier to maintain. Comma-separated.
# GONG_TEAM_USER_IDS=1234567890,9876543210
# GONG_TEAM_EMAILS=ae@yourco.com,sdr@yourco.com,cs@yourco.com

# Optional: per-request timeout against the Gong API, in milliseconds.
# GONG_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# Optional: override the Gong API host. The default global host routes to the
# right cell for your credentials; set this if Gong's dashboard shows a
# company-specific host and the default does not work. Either form is accepted.
# GONG_API_BASE_URL=https://us-18795.api.gong.io
